    <td height="25" valign="top" class="mainLeftMenu">The Journal</td>
    <td rowspan="6" valign="top" class="mainCellContents"><p align="justify" class="mainCellContents">ECJ 
        was founded in September 2003 by then-Stanford IEEE President Clara Shih. 
        During her three-year tenure as President, Shih had noticed a gap between 
        current undergraduate research presentation opportunities and highly specialized 
        technical journals largely dominated by graduate students, post-doctoral 
        students, and faculty.</p>
      <p class="mainCellContents">&quot;I began investigating what other schools 
        were doing to address this need and found that many of our peer institutions, 
        namely Berkeley and MIT, had successfully launched EE/CS research journals 
        in recent years,&quot; said Shih. &quot;I felt it was only appropriate 
        that Stanford, with such a strong tradition and emphasis on research, 
        particularly in technical fields, should consider doing the same, so I 
        proceeded to rally support from peers, faculty and administration.&quot;</p>
      <p class="mainCellContents">Established in 
        autumn quarter last year, the ECJ steering committee comprises undergraduates, 
        graduate students and faculty. The committee decided early-on that ECJ 
        would feature both &quot;short papers&quot; and &quot;long papers,&quot; 
        to accommodate a wide range of both authors and audiences. Short papers, 
        which are meant as surveys of a particular topic for an audience of technical 
        laypersons, are reviewed by three members of the ECJ steering committee, 
        along with an optional faculty review. Long papers, such as those in more 
        traditional journals, undergo more rigorous review. After an initial screening 
        by the steering committee, papers are categorized and sent off anonymously 
        to faculty, post-doctorate and IEEE professional volunteers knowledgeable 
        about research in their respective fields. Based on a weighted average 
        of the review &quot;scores&quot;, the steering committee makes the final 
        publication and scholarship selections.</span><br>
      </p></td>
  </tr>
